<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivor Wilks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a>–<a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ivor G. Wilks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivor Wilks was a British Africanist and historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, considered one of the founders of modern African historiography. After service in the British Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and studies at Bangor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, he went in 1953 to the University College of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, now the University of Ghana, where he devoted his career to what he called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> of West African history and helped set up the Institute of African Studies at Legon. In 1966 he moved to Northwestern University. His 1975 book on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> in the nineteenth century remains a standard text on the Ashanti Empire; he also wrote on the Welsh working-class movement.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivor G. Wilks was born on 19 July 1928. In the 1940s he served as a lieutenant in the British Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. At Bangor University he was an ardent supporter of Welsh independence and took part in Welsh nationalist politics and the Welsh Republican Movement. He graduated in 1951 and went on to take a degree in philosophy at Oxford University.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the University College of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, which later became the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, Wilks studied the nature of power and leadership and the patterns of collaboration and resistance in West African history. He remained at Northwestern University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> from 1966 until his retirement, was Herskovits Professor of African Studies there, and was Professor Emeritus of History at his death on 7 October 2014. He received the ASA Distinguished Africanist Award in 1998. He was married to Grace O. Amanor and, from 1989, to Nancy Lawler, and had four children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Wilks wrote 178 published works. His first book, The Northern Factor in Ashanti History (1961), was published by the Institute of African Studies at Legon. Asante in the Nineteenth Century: The Structure and Evolution of a Political Order appeared from Cambridge University Press in 1975 and is regarded as a classic of Africanist scholarship.</p><p>His later books include Chronicles from Gonja: A West African Tradition of Muslim Historiography (1986, with N. Levtzion and B. Haight), Wa and the Wala: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Polity in Northwestern Ghana (1989), Forests of Gold: Essays on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> (1993), One Nation, Many Histories: Ghana past and present (1996) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a> 1640–1750: a Study of the Rise and Fall of a West African Empire (2001). His work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> and the Rising of 1839: Class Struggle as Armed Struggle (1984).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivor Wilks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
